  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "This is the thin lens equation used in the study of optics.\n\n$$\\frac{1}{u}+\\frac{1}{v}=\\frac{1}{f}$$\n\nWhere $f$ is the focal length of a lens, $u$ is distance to the image, and $v$ is distance to the object.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $u=\\frac{vf}{v-f}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$u=\\frac{vf}{v-f}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{u}&=\\frac{1}{f}-\\frac{1}{v}\\\\\\frac{1}{u}&=\\frac{v-f}{fv}\\\\u&=\\frac{vf}{v-f}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Briefly explain why $v\\ne f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $v=f$, then $v-f=0$, and you cannot divide by zero.",
          "worked": "If $v=f$, the denominator in $u=\\frac{vf}{v-f}$ is zero. Division by zero is undefined, so $v\\ne f$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Let $x_1=u-f$ and $x_2=v-f$. Show that $x_1x_2=f^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x_1x_2=f^2$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x_1x_2&=(u-f)(v-f)\\\\x_1x_2&=uv-fu-fv+f^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitute $u=\\frac{vf}{v-f}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x_1x_2&=\\left(\\frac{vf}{v-f}\\right)v-f\\left(\\frac{vf}{v-f}\\right)-fv+f^2\\\\x_1x_2&=\\frac{v^2f}{v-f}-\\frac{f^2v}{v-f}-fv+f^2\\\\x_1x_2&=\\frac{vf(v-f)}{v-f}-fv+f^2\\\\x_1x_2&=vf-fv+f^2\\\\x_1x_2&=f^2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
